		<!-- THE LIMITS: what these numbers are not -->
		<section class="relative w-full" data-reveal>
			<ZineTape Variant="section" Label="LIMITS // WHAT THE NUMBERS ARE NOT" />
			<ZineBox Size="lg" Rotate="-rotate-[0.9deg]">
				<h2
					id="limits"
					class="mb-8 font-headline text-2xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-4xl"
				>
					Where the figures stop
				</h2>
				<div class="space-y-5">
					<ZineLesson
						Label="NOT A TYPICAL CASE"
						Color="raw"
						Text={`${Benchmark.Ceiling} is the ceiling on one long low-entropy document. The per-type chart peaks at ${Peak.Ratio}, and the median the same table reports is ${Benchmark.Median} - quote the ceiling only with the corpus attached.`}
					/>
					<ZineLesson
						Label="NOT A SINGLE PERCENTAGE"
						Color="canary"
						Text={`The session saving is a band, not a point: ${Study.Band} across a ${Study.CharsPerTok} chars/token sensitivity range, centered ${Study.BandCenter}. Any single number inside that band is a reading of the same data, not a better measurement.`}
					/>
					<ZineLesson
						Label="NOT REAL USAGE"
						Color="teal"
						Text={`The ${Study.Bands[3]!.Range} pure-tool figure comes from a simulated diff+ls+build+search loop. It is a stress test of the engine, and the real sessions it sits beside saved ${Study.Bands[2]!.Range} when they were tool-heavy.`}
					/>
					<ZineLesson
						Label="NOT NET OF RETRIEVAL"
						Color="canary"
						Text={`Savings are counted before retrieval. The study's own tax row - ${Study.TaxCalls} calls, ${Study.TaxTokens} tokens - is what the model spent getting bytes back, roughly ${Study.TaxPct} of the tokens saved.`}
					/>
				</div>
				<p class="mt-10 border-t border-oxblood-dark pt-8 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
					None of this weakens the claim the page makes.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
					A compression proxy earns its place on the ordinary turn - a diff, a build log, a
					terminal dump - where the ratio is {Floor.Ratio} to {RunnerUp.Ratio} and the model keeps working from
					the preview.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-3 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
					The ceiling is what the engine can do; the median is what a session gets.
